# Domain guide: smartphones

## What actually matters

- **Performance**: Chipset (e.g., Snapdragon 8 Gen 3, A17 Pro) and RAM (8–16 GB typical). Benchmarks (Geekbench, AnTuTu) matter less than real-world use (gaming, multitasking, longevity).
- **Display**: Size (6.1–6.9"), resolution (FHD+ to QHD+), refresh rate (60–144 Hz), brightness (1000+ nits for sunlight), and panel type (OLED vs. LCD). LTPO for adaptive refresh is a plus.
- **Battery life**: Capacity (3000–5000 mAh) and efficiency (chipset + software). Real-world endurance (e.g., "2 days of moderate use") beats mAh numbers.
- **Charging**: Wired (25–120W) and wireless (5–50W) speeds. Reverse wireless charging is niche.
- **Cameras**: Sensor size (1/1.3" or larger is better), aperture (f/1.5–f/2.4), and computational photography (night mode, HDR). Video (4K/8K, stabilization) matters for creators.
- **Software**: OS version (Android 14, iOS 17) and update policy (3–7 years of updates). iOS offers longer support; Android varies by brand.
- **Build**: Materials (glass, metal, plastic), IP rating (IP68 for dust/water resistance), and durability (Gorilla Glass Victus 2 or equivalent).
- **5G/connectivity**: Bands supported (check local carrier compatibility). Wi-Fi 6/6E and Bluetooth 5.3+ are standard.
- **Storage**: 128–512 GB base; expandable storage (microSD) is rare but useful.
- **Repairability**: Ease of battery/screen replacement (iFixit scores). Apple and Google score poorly; Fairphone is best.

## Sources

- **Lab tests**: Display (DisplayMate), battery (GSMArena), camera (DxOMark), and performance (AnandTech).
- **Specialist reviewers**: Tech YouTubers (Marques Brownlee, Mrwhosetheboss) and sites (The Verge, Android Authority, GSMArena).
- **Owner communities**: Reddit (r/Android, r/iPhone, r/GooglePixel), XDA Developers, and brand-specific forums.
- **Retailers**: Local carrier/retailer specs for regional variants (e.g., US vs. EU models).

## Pitfalls

- **Regional differences**: Bands (5G, LTE), charging standards (USB-C vs. proprietary), and software (e.g., Samsung Pay vs. Google Pay).
- **Model confusion**: "Pro" vs. "Ultra" vs. "Max" variants differ in size, battery, and cameras. Confirm the exact model.
- **Misleading specs**: Megapixels ≠ camera quality; "100x zoom" is often digital (useless). Check real-world samples.
- **Update policies**: Android brands vary widely (Google/Samsung: 5–7 years; others: 2–3 years). iOS is consistent.
- **Bloatware**: Carrier/brand skins (e.g., Samsung One UI, Xiaomi MIUI) can slow updates or add ads. Stock Android (Pixel) is cleanest.
